# Line endings for TwinCAT's own file families.
#
# TwinCAT writes PLC object files as UTF-8 **with a BOM** and **CRLF** line endings, and both are
# load-bearing here: `scripts/Create sample PLC project/build-sample-project.js` (writeTwinCatFile)
# emits them that way on purpose, the LSP's offset handling assumes them, and several harnesses
# read the real sample and assert against `\r\n` anchors.
#
# Without these rules the CRLF survived only by accident. Git's default on Windows
# (core.autocrlf=true) converts LF to CRLF on checkout, so the committed sample — which is stored
# with LF — LOOKED correct on the machine it was developed on, while a Linux or macOS clone got LF
# and `test/browser/build.js` failed with "FB_Cylinder no longer starts the way the fixture
# expects". Pinning eol=crlf makes the working tree identical on every platform regardless of a
# developer's core.autocrlf setting, which is the only thing TwinCAT and the harnesses ever read.
#
# These stay `text` (not `-text`) so they still diff line-by-line in review; git normalizes to LF in
# the object database and restores CRLF on checkout.

*.TcPOU     text eol=crlf
*.TcGVL     text eol=crlf
*.TcDUT     text eol=crlf
*.TcIO      text eol=crlf
*.TcTTO     text eol=crlf
*.TcVIS     text eol=crlf
*.TcVMO     text eol=crlf
*.TcTLO     text eol=crlf
*.TcGTLO    text eol=crlf
*.TcIPO     text eol=crlf

# The XAE-authored solution skeleton. Visual Studio writes these CRLF, and the generator deliberately
# preserves whatever XAE produced rather than rewriting it.
*.sln       text eol=crlf
*.tsproj    text eol=crlf
*.plcproj   text eol=crlf
*.xti       text eol=crlf
*.xtv       text eol=crlf
*.tmc       text eol=crlf

# Library archives are ZIP containers (and `.compiled-library-v3` is an opaque binary format). Any
# eol conversion would corrupt them and take the ZIP reader's harnesses down with it.
*.library                   binary
*.compiled-library          binary
*.compiled-library-ge33     binary
*.compiled-library-v3       binary

# PowerShell is executed by Windows hosts; keep it CRLF.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
